\begin_layout Standard
There are two budgets 
\begin_inset Formula $B_1=(p^1,m_1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B_2=(p^2,m_2)$
\end_inset

, with 
\begin_inset Formula $m_1=m_2=12$
\end_inset

. A consumer's stable type is denoted by 
\begin_inset Formula $\nu$
\end_inset

, demand by 
\begin_inset Formula $q(p,m;\nu)$
\end_inset

, and indirect utility by 
\begin_inset Formula $v(p,m;\nu)$
\end_inset

. Write 
\begin_inset Formula \[
q^1_\nu=q(p^1,m_1;\nu),\; q^2_\nu=q(p^2,m_2;\nu).
\]
\end_inset

For two cross-sections, define the cross-affordability shares 
\begin_inset Formula \[
r_{12}=\Pr\!\left[m_1\ge p^1\!\cdot q^2_\nu\right],
\;
r_{21}=\Pr\!\left[m_2\ge p^2\!\cdot q^1_\nu\right].
\]
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{12}$
\end_inset

 is the share of types whose choice under budget 2 would also have been affordable under budget 1. Assume no cross-expenditure equality and no welfare tie has positive probability. Individual utility maximization implies 
\begin_inset Formula \[
r_{12}\le \theta_{12}\le 1-r_{21},
\;
\theta_{12}:=\Pr\!\left[v(p^1,m_1;\nu)>v(p^2,m_2;\nu)\right],
\]
\end_inset

and hence the pairwise 
\series bold
Weak Axiom of Stochastic Revealed Preference (WASRP)
\series default
: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
r_{12}+r_{21}\le 1.
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The population has five equally likely types. Their cross expenditures are: 
\begin_inset Formula \[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\toprule
\text{type }\nu & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5\\
\midrule
p^1\!\cdot q^2_\nu & 9 & 10.5 & 11.25 & 13.5 & 13.5\\
p^2\!\cdot q^1_\nu & 15 & 13.5 & 12.6 & 9 & 15\\
\bottomrule
\end{array}
\]
\end_inset


\end_layout

